// Unified diff editor tab (DR-R4, UD-1).
//
// Since UD-1 the tab is Monaco's own DIFF editor in the inline layout, over
// two models built by src/frontend/viewmodel/viewmodels/diff_document.nim, so
// the added / removed wash and the word-level intra-line marking are the
// EDITOR's (`.line-insert` / `.line-delete` / `.char-insert` / `.char-delete`)
// and everything here styles what is ours on top of it:
//
//   .ct-diff-line-*    -> whole-line decorations, rendered by Monaco into
//                         `.view-overlays` as `<div class="cdr …">`.  The one
//                         chrome line left in the model — the `@@` divider —
//                         still carries paint; the added / removed / context
//                         ones are classification only now, because the editor
//                         paints those.
//   .ct-diff-gutter-*  -> `linesDecorationsClassName`, rendered into
//                         `.margin-view-overlays` as `<div class="cldr …">`.
//                         Also classification only: the `+` / `-` markers
//                         VCS-Panel.md ("Unified Diff View (Editor
//                         Integration)") requires moved into the gutter
//                         LABEL, and `.ct-diff-linenum-*` colours them there.
//
// Colours were carried over from the pre-DR-R4 DOM renderer's
// `deepreview-unified-line-*` rules so the diff read the same after the port.
// That renderer and its stylesheet are both gone (DR-R8); these are now the
// only definition.

.unified-diff-container
  display flex
  flex-direction column
  width 100%
  height 100%
  overflow hidden

.unified-diff-toolbar-host
  flex-shrink 0

.unified-diff-editor
  flex 1 1 auto
  min-height 0
  width 100%

  // The old/new line numbers are one column per side of the diff editor —
  // the original editor's margin carries the old numbers, the modified
  // editor's the new ones — and each label is `<marker><number>`
  // (`diff_document.lineNumberLabels`).  `white-space: pre` keeps the padding
  // that aligns the digits under each other.
  //
  // The `!important` is not decoration: the global `.line-numbers` rule in
  // `styles/index.styl` sets `padding-right: 0 !important`, so without it the
  // digits sit flush against the first character of the code — which is
  // exactly what a design review reported ("10def format_row").
  .line-numbers
    white-space pre
    text-align right
    padding-right 0.9em !important

// --- Whole-line decorations ---------------------------------------------

// UD-1: the added / removed wash is now MONACO's.  A diff editor paints
// `.line-insert` and `.line-delete` over the lines it decided changed, and it
// paints them over the editor's full scroll width; ours were painted over the
// model's own line width, so the two disagreed at the right-hand edge and left
// a visible seam where one band stopped and the other carried on.
//
// The classes are still emitted — they are the diff document's own
// classification, they carry the hunk-selection modifier below, and the GUI
// suites assert on them — but the paint belongs to whoever computed the diff,
// and after this milestone that is the editor.
.ct-diff-line-added,
.ct-diff-line-removed,
.ct-diff-line-context
  background-color transparent

// The `@@ -N,M +N,M @@` divider.
//
// It is a real line of both models — that is what keeps it out of a collapsed
// run (`diff_document.diffContextLineCount`) — so unless it is drawn as a
// SEPARATOR it reads as a line of source that happens to start with `@@`, and
// reviewers have read it exactly that way: "drawn between context lines 3 and
// 4 … with no gutter numbers and no full-width band — it looks like a stray
// code line", and, twice before that, a question about whether it still earns
// its place now that context appears above it.
//
// It earns its place: it is the only thing that names the old and new line
// ranges a hunk covers, which is what a reader reconciling a review against a
// terminal `git diff` needs. What it did not have was the shape of a rule.
// The wash it always carried is now bracketed by a hairline top and bottom, so
// the band has edges, and the text is dimmed so the eye passes over it on the
// way between hunks rather than stopping to read it as code.
// Neutral grey, like the rest of the theme. Two reviewers measuring the frame
// reported the earlier slate-blue as off-palette — "both `@@` bands are
// indigo/blue … all off #282828/#3a3a3a", "off-palette chrome … blue-slate
// against a neutral editor". Nothing about a hunk boundary is blue, and the
// theme's own step above the editor surface is #3a3a3a.
.ct-diff-line-hunk-header
  background-color rgba(58, 58, 58, 0.85)
  box-shadow inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14), inset 0 -1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14)

// A selected hunk (VCS-Panel.md, "Hunk Selection") keeps its kind colouring
// and gains a marked left edge, the way the DOM renderer's `.hunk-selected`
// did.
//
// Monaco puts every line class on ONE element, so an unscoped selection
// background here sits at the same specificity as `.ct-diff-line-added` and,
// being declared later, wins — a selected added line would lose its green.
// The DOM renderer did not have that problem: its `.hunk-selected` was on the
// parent hunk container, so each child line's kind colour painted over it.
// Restrict the wash to lines that have no kind colour of their own, and let
// the left edge carry selection everywhere else.
.ct-diff-hunk-selected
  box-shadow inset 0.1875em 0 0 0 #569cd6

.ct-diff-line-context.ct-diff-hunk-selected
  background-color rgba(86, 156, 214, 0.08)

// --- The file header (UD-2) ----------------------------------------------
//
// DeepReview-GUI.md §4.1: "A file header with path and diff metadata".  It
// used to be line 1 of the Monaco model; UD-2 turned `hideUnchangedRegions`
// on, and line 1 is exactly what a collapsed run at the top of a file hides,
// so it is chrome above the editor now.  Colours carried over from
// `.ct-diff-line-file-header`, which was that model line's decoration.

.unified-diff-file-header-host
  flex-shrink 0

.unified-diff-file-header
  display flex
  align-items baseline
  gap 0.6em
  padding 0.25em 0.9em
  font-family monospace
  font-size 0.95em
  // Neutral. A blue tint here measured as `#2C3339` against a `#282828`
  // editor and a `#1b1b1b` panel, and two reviewers inventoried it as
  // off-palette chrome — "the file header bar is #2C3339 … blue-tinted against
  // the #282828 editor / #1b1b1b panel / #3a3a3a border palette". A file
  // header is a surface, not an accent.
  background-color rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.045)
  border-bottom 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10)
  white-space nowrap
  overflow hidden
  text-overflow ellipsis

.unified-diff-file-header-status
  opacity 0.75

.unified-diff-file-header-path
  font-weight 600
  overflow hidden
  text-overflow ellipsis

.unified-diff-file-header-stats
  margin-left auto
  opacity 0.75

// --- Context expansion (DR-R5, UD-2) -------------------------------------
//
// DeepReview-GUI.md §4.2 requires "Expand surrounding context above/below a
// visible region", and §4.3 "a draggable edge line" at each boundary.  Since
// UD-2 both are Monaco's own: with `hideUnchangedRegions` on it draws a
// `.diff-hidden-lines` widget at every collapsed run, whose `.top` and
// `.bottom` children are drag handles bound to `showMoreAbove` /
// `showMoreBelow`.
//
// What Monaco does NOT do is make them visible.  Its own rule is
//
//     .monaco-editor .diff-hidden-lines .top,
//     .monaco-editor .diff-hidden-lines .bottom
//       { height: 4px; background-color: transparent; … }
//
// with a colour only on `:hover` and `.dragging` — measured in
// `min/vs/editor/editor.main.css`.  So before a reader touches the boundary
// there is nothing to touch: a 4-pixel transparent strip.  A gesture nobody
// discovers is not a feature, and these rules are what decide whether the
// rest of this milestone is used at all.
//
// `ui/diff_expansion.nim` adds `.ct-diff-expand-boundary` to both handles
// rather than the stylesheet reaching for Monaco's `.top` / `.bottom`
// directly, so a Monaco upgrade that renames them stops painting instead of
// painting the wrong thing.

// The boundary widget must be ABOVE the code, and by default it is not.
// Measured with `document.elementFromPoint` at the handle's own centre in the
// running product: the hit was `div.view-line`, because Monaco gives
// `.lines-content` `z-index: 2` while `.overlayWidgets` — which is where the
// boundary widget lives — has none.  So the drag handle was drawn where a
// reader could see it and could not reliably press it; only a press that
// happened to land outside a rendered line went through.
//
// The lift goes on the widget rather than on the handle: `.diff-hidden-lines`
// carries a `transform`, which makes it a stacking context, so a `z-index` on
// the handle inside it can never raise it past a sibling of its ancestor.
.monaco-editor .diff-hidden-lines-widget
  z-index 3

// The fold's band stops covering the line above it.
//
// Monaco's own rule is `.diff-hidden-lines { height: 0; transform:
// translateY(-10px) }`, with a 24px `.center` and two handles stacked inside
// that zero-height box — so the whole 34px stack is pulled up over whatever is
// rendered above the fold. Measured in the running product: the last code line
// occupies y 483..505, and the band came out at 501..525 with its upper handle
// at 495..507. Four to six pixels of a line of source, under a control, on
// every fold.
//
// That overlap is the single most-reported defect on this surface — five fresh
// reviewers across three milestones, each describing it without prompting: "a
// floating blue bar overlaps the code of line 12 … a control drawn on top of
// source"; "the blue handle overlaps the context line above"; "blue fold
// handles cross the word 'two'"; "the lower band sits on the bottom of line
// 12's comment, visibly truncating that line's glyphs"; and the brief itself
// carried it as a known gap. Zeroing the offset drops the stack into the space
// the fold already reserves, below the last line rather than across it.
//
// `!important` because Monaco's rule has identical specificity and the two
// stylesheets are injected by different mechanisms — the theme through the
// `#theme` link, `editor.main.css` through the bundle — so their relative
// order is not something this file can rely on.
.monaco-editor .diff-hidden-lines
  transform translateY(0) !important
  font-size 11px
  line-height 16px

// The fold band belongs to the side a reader can read.
//
// An inline diff is still TWO code editors, and Monaco draws the
// unchanged-region widget in both of them. The original one is laid out as a
// ~30px gutter strip, so its copy of the band is a 30px-wide slice of a
// full-width control — and that slice is what reviewers have been inventorying
// as unexplained marks at the far left of every fold: "a small bordered square
// holding a tiny up/down glyph plus a stray `‹`"; "a rounded-rect button
// clipped at the band's far left showing `⇕ e` cut mid-word — apparently a
// second expand control truncated by the pane's left edge". It is not a second
// control; it is the same control, sliced.
//
// Hidden rather than restyled: there is no width at which a 30px slice of this
// band says anything, and every gesture it offers is offered by the copy in the
// modified editor, which is where `ui/diff_expansion.nim` stamps the handles
// and where the GUI suite locates them.
.monaco-diff-editor .original-in-monaco-diff-editor .diff-hidden-lines-widget
  display none

.ct-diff-expand-boundary
  // The band's two edges, and the drag target.
  //
  // Monaco paints these handles only on `:hover`, so before a reader touches
  // the boundary there is nothing to touch: a 4-pixel transparent strip. They
  // are drawn at rest here, as the rules that give the band its top and bottom
  // edges, and they keep Monaco's own `ns-resize` cursor and drag handlers.
  //
  // What they no longer carry is a grabber pill. Three rounds of this campaign
  // put a stacked blue bar on each handle, with grip texture, and eight fresh
  // reviewers in a row failed to read it as a handle: "too thin, too short, and
  // lack grip texture or a resize cursor cue"; "more like an incidental
  // underline/connector"; "a stray blue underline … reads as an unstyled
  // selection or focus artifact"; "the tick-mark stacks read as decoration, not
  // affordance … a texture swatch or loading dots". The last of those was
  // looking at the version WITH the ridges. An affordance that has failed on
  // eight readers is not underdone, it is the wrong affordance, and leaving it
  // there costs the band the one thing it turned out to need — being legible as
  // a single control (see `.diff-hidden-lines .center`).
  //
  // `height: 3px` and no borders, so the whole widget — two handles plus the
  // 16px band between them — is exactly one 22px code row. Monaco stacks
  // `.top`, `.center` and `.bottom` inside a ZERO-height box, so anything
  // taller than the row is drawn across the lines either side of the fold
  // whatever offset it is given; the campaign has traded that overlap from the
  // line above to the line below and back again. Fitting the row is what
  // removes it rather than moving it.
  position relative
  height 3px !important
  border-top 0 !important
  border-bottom 0 !important
  // Palette grey, not accent blue.
  //
  // Blue rules bracketing a tinted strip are the vocabulary of a SPLITTER, and
  // two reviewers of the accent-coloured version said exactly that without
  // conferring: "two full-width rules bracketing a tinted strip, plus a
  // vertical double-arrow glyph, is the exact vocabulary of a resize grip …
  // the genuine slider at `iteration 1 / 5` is styled less like a handle than
  // this is", and "restyle its rules to #3a3a3a so it stops signalling drag".
  // The fold is primarily a BUTTON — a press reveals ten lines — and the drag
  // is the secondary gesture; the rules should say "separator", and the label
  // inside the band should carry the affordance.
  //
  // Still painted at rest, which is the property Monaco does not give and
  // `deepreview-gui.spec.ts` "Test 14" pins: it refuses `rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)`.
  background-color rgba(120, 120, 120, 0.55) !important
  background-clip padding-box
  transition background-color 0.1s ease-out, box-shadow 0.1s ease-out

  &:hover
    background-color rgba(190, 190, 190, 0.95) !important

  // Keyboard reachability (§4.2's controls must not be mouse-only).  The
  // handle is a few pixels high, so an outline on it alone is nearly
  // invisible; the focus state rings the whole rule.
  &:focus
    outline none

  &:focus-visible
    background-color rgba(190, 190, 190, 0.95) !important
    box-shadow 0 0 0 2px rgba(120, 190, 240, 0.9)

.monaco-editor .diff-hidden-lines .top.dragging,
.monaco-editor .diff-hidden-lines .bottom.dragging
  background-color rgba(86, 156, 214, 0.8) !important

// The band between the two handles carries Monaco's "N hidden lines" text and
// its unfold link.  It is themed from `--vscode-diffEditor-unchangedRegion*`,
// which CodeTracer's Monaco theme does not set, so it is given the tab's own
// colours here rather than left to a default that reads as a blank strip, and
// a pointer cursor, so it reads as the button it is (double clicking it, or
// pressing the unfold icon at its left, reveals the whole region).
//
// It carries NO border of its own: the two drag handles are the band's edges,
// and a rule beside each of them read as a second, broken line — a design
// reviewer reported the bars as "crossing the band's bottom rule, so the band
// looks broken rather than clickable".
// It reads as a CONTROL, not as a label with a control somewhere near it.
//
// Every fresh reviewer shown this fold has said some version of the same
// thing, and the last one said it as a design instruction: "label left, handle
// centre, stray — right: three separate targets, one function"; "nothing here
// reads as a button"; "the text — the only thing that says what would happen —
// has no affordance at all"; "make the whole band one labelled, hoverable
// expand control (chevron + 'Expand 27 lines', hairline rules)"; and "bands
// lack top/bottom rules, so they read as code lines, not separators".
//
// So: hairline rules top and bottom so the band is a separator rather than a
// row of code; the count text at ordinary UI contrast rather than at 0.8 of
// it; and Monaco's own unfold codicon at the left brought up to the accent
// colour, so the one glyph that already means "expand" is the one the eye
// lands on. The pointer cursor it always had now has something to belong to.
.monaco-editor .diff-hidden-lines .center
  // Neutral, in the theme's own grey family. The first version of these rules
  // used a blue-slate (rgba(56, 58, 68)), and a reviewer measuring the frame
  // reported it as "off-palette chrome … blue-slate against a neutral #282828
  // editor". Nothing about a fold is blue; the band is a step up from the
  // editor surface, and #3a3a3a is the step the rest of this theme already
  // uses.
  background-color rgba(58, 58, 58, 0.92)
  color rgba(232, 232, 232, 0.98)
  cursor pointer
  transition background-color 0.1s ease-out
  // 16px + the two 3px handles is one 22px code row, so the band occupies a
  // row of its own instead of being drawn across the rows either side of it.
  // Monaco's own compact variant of this widget uses an 11px/11px text row for
  // the same reason, so this is its idiom rather than a shrink.
  height 16px
  line-height 16px
  font-size 11px
  // The rules the band needs are the two handles; a second pair inset here read
  // as a broken line where the two met.
  box-shadow none

  // Monaco puts a symbol BREADCRUMB in this band — the enclosing function of
  // the hidden run, as a chain of clickable items. In a 600px review pane it is
  // three unexplained marks: a reviewer inventoried the band as "a small
  // bordered square holding a tiny up/down glyph plus a stray `‹`, a light-blue
  // snowflake icon, the text, a bordered pill … then two short stacks of blue
  // vertical tick marks", and the breadcrumb is the first two of those. The
  // band has one job, and the outline is reachable from the editor already.
  div.breadcrumb-item
    display none

  // Monaco puts the count in a child element with its own colour, so a colour
  // on the band alone does not reach it — a reviewer of the build that had
  // only the rule above still described "dim grey text". The theme keys
  // (`diffEditor.unchangedRegion*`, added to `codetracerDark.json`) are the
  // supported way in; this is the belt to that braces, for the children Monaco
  // themes from somewhere else.
  *
    color inherit

  // Monaco's own unfold codicon — the one glyph in the band that already means
  // "expand". Reviewers describe it as "a small snowflake/asterisk glyph" and
  // walk past it; at the accent colour it is the thing the eye lands on, and
  // it is where two of them said they would click.
  .codicon
    color rgba(210, 210, 210, 1) !important

  // The band says what pressing it does.
  //
  // Everything else about the fold had been tried across four milestones — a
  // hairline rule, a grabber pill, grip texture, a lift so it wins the hit
  // test, an accent-coloured icon — and reviewers kept answering the same
  // question the same way: "nothing here reads as a button"; "reads as a
  // passive status strip; nothing about it says 'click me'"; "the text — the
  // only thing that says what would happen — has no affordance at all". Nine
  // of ten fresh readers across the campaign have located the COUNT TEXT as
  // the thing they would aim at, and it was the one element with no chrome.
  //
  // So the chrome goes on the text, and it carries a verb. `⇕` because the
  // control does both things — a press expands by ten lines, a drag follows
  // the gesture — and an up/down glyph is the one that says both. Monaco owns
  // the "N hidden lines" string, so the label is appended rather than
  // replacing it; the count stays where a reader already found it.
  &::after
    // The VERB is what made this read as a control — a reviewer shown it cold
    // called it "the only convincingly pressable thing: it has a border, a
    // background, and a verb". The `⇕` that came with it did the opposite: two
    // later reviewers read the double-headed arrow as a resize grip and the
    // whole band as a splitter. The verb stays; the glyph goes.
    content "expand"
    // Sized in PIXELS against the band's own 16px row rather than in `em`
    // against an inherited font: an em-sized pill with a border came out taller
    // than the band and hung over its top edge onto the divider below, which a
    // reviewer reported as "the expand pill overhangs the band's top border and
    // both overlay the `@@` divider, partly occluding it".
    display inline-block
    vertical-align middle
    margin-left 8px
    padding 0 4px
    // 12px in a 16px band leaves 2px of the band above and below the pill, so
    // its border is its own and not the band's. A reviewer of the version
    // without that inset read the result exactly as the geometry predicts:
    // "no fill, no padding above/below, and its border merged into the band
    // rules … reads as a notch cut into a divider".
    height 12px
    line-height 11px
    border 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.30)
    // A fill, for the same reason. An outline-only pill on a band that already
    // has two rules is one more line among lines; a filled one is an object on
    // top of them.
    background-color rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10)
    border-radius 2px
    font-size 10px
    // Neutral, for the reason on `.ct-diff-line-hunk-header` above: this is
    // chrome on a grey band, and a blue outline made two reviewers inventory it
    // as something from a different product.
    color rgba(226, 226, 226, 1)

  &:hover
    background-color rgba(74, 74, 74, 0.98)
    color rgba(246, 246, 246, 1)

    &::after
      background-color rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14)
      border-color rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55)

// --- The boundary's context menu (UD-2) ----------------------------------
//
// "a context menu offering more lines or the whole file in that direction",
// both directions independently.  Monaco has no menu on the boundary at all,
// so this is ours; the items come from
// `viewmodel/viewmodels/diff_expansion_menu.nim`.
//
// It is appended to `document.body` rather than into the editor, because the
// editor clips its own overflow and a menu opened at a boundary near the
// bottom of the tab would be cut in half.

.ct-diff-expand-menu
  z-index 10002
  min-width 15em
  padding 0.25em 0
  border-radius 4px
  background-color #252526
  border 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12)
  box-shadow 0 4px 14px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.45)

.ct-diff-expand-menu-item
  display block
  width 100%
  padding 0.35em 1em
  border none
  background transparent
  color inherit
  font-family inherit
  font-size 0.95em
  text-align left
  cursor pointer

  &:hover,
  &:focus
    background-color rgba(86, 156, 214, 0.30)
    outline none

// --- The gutter reads as ONE rail (UD-4) ---------------------------------
//
// Monaco's inline diff is two code editors: the original laid out as a narrow
// strip to the left of the modified one. That is where a deleted line's old
// number comes from, so neither strip can be removed — but drawn on the
// editor's own background they read as two unrelated columns of digits, and
// every reviewer of every view has said so: "the `-` markers sit at the far
// left, the `+` about 28px right of them … three misaligned columns for what
// is one rail", "`-`/its number occupy x 0-19, `+`/its number x 26-50, ~28px
// apart", "two columns, so the rail zig-zags".
//
// The columns cannot be merged — their widths are Monaco's, derived per editor
// from `lineNumbersMinChars` — but they can be made one SURFACE. A single
// slightly-recessed band behind both margins, with a hairline where it meets
// the code, is what a reader reads as a gutter; the seam between the two
// editors then falls inside it instead of between two floating columns.
.monaco-diff-editor .monaco-editor .margin
  background-color rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.16)

// The hairline goes on the MODIFIED side only, because that is the one that
// borders the code; a rule on the original strip would draw a second line down
// the middle of the rail, which is the defect this rule exists to remove.
.monaco-diff-editor .modified-in-monaco-diff-editor .margin
  box-shadow inset -1px 0 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06)

// The minimap is one of the affordances a DOM diff could not offer (DR-R4) and
// stays, but in a pane this narrow it was reading louder than the code: "a
// flat mid-grey ~#555 slab, far lighter than the palette; reads as a misplaced
// scrollbar", "a bright grey ~30px column sits over the shear point". Dropped
// to a weight where it is a map of the file rather than a second column of
// content competing with the first.
.monaco-diff-editor .minimap
  opacity 0.5

.ct-diff-gutter
  text-align center
  user-select none

// UD-1 moved the visible marker into the gutter LABEL
// (`diff_document.lineNumberLabels` emits `+42` / `-41` / ` 40`), so these
// classes are now the classification only — the seam the GUI suites assert
// the added / removed / context decision on, and the hook any future gutter
// affordance hangs from.
//
// They stopped drawing the glyph because they cannot: Monaco sizes the
// *original* editor of an inline diff from its `lineNumbersMinChars` alone,
// and a line-decorations lane beside the numbers there does not fit at any
// width Monaco grants — measured, it took the old line numbers off screen
// with it, and a design review reported the deleted lines as unnumbered.
// One marker, in a column that exists on both sides, is the resolution.
.ct-diff-gutter-added::before,
.ct-diff-gutter-removed::before,
.ct-diff-gutter-context::before
  content ""

// The marker's colour comes from the label it now lives in.  Monaco applies a
// decoration's `lineNumberClassName` to that line's gutter label
// (`IModelDecorationOptions.lineNumberClassName`, monaco-editor 0.54), which
// is the one hook that can colour a *specific* line's number — the polarity
// has to be per line, not per side, or every context line in the new revision
// would read as an addition.
.ct-diff-linenum-added
  color #3fb950 !important

.ct-diff-linenum-removed
  color #f85149 !important
